# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: doc-build-info-export-quadratic — the Info export of the spec is quadratic: 51 minutes of a 55-minute documentation job
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* Context

pjb, 2026-08-19, after two merge requests each spent about an hour in the
=documentation= job: cache the Info export separately from the HTML split
— "mais il faut vérifier. Je pense que ce qui est lent, c'est le split du
gros .org". He proposed a reorganisation: one =.org= per dictionary entry,
so that editing one page regenerates one =.info= and one =.html= and the
rest is essentially =cat *.org > big.org=, which is fast.

This issue records the measurements he asked for, because the first half
of that guess turns out to be wrong and the conclusion right anyway.

* Where the time actually goes — measured, not estimated

From the trace of job 15981142889 (=documentation=, MR !105, 60.2 min,
SUCCESS — it was slow, not stuck):

| step                          | duration    | volume     |
|-------------------------------+-------------+------------|
| =build-paged-spec= (text)     | *1.9 s*     | 3022 pages |
| =build-paged-spec= (org)      | *3.1 s*     | 3022 pages |
| =build-paged-html=            | *2 min 38 s*| 3022 pages |
| =build-paged-info= (texi)     | *51 min 19 s* | 1 file   |

*The split is not the slow part.* It costs five seconds for both formats
together. The single =org-texinfo-export-to-texinfo= call on the whole
95 000-line spec is 94% of the job.

Worth stating plainly because it changes what to fix: caching the Info
export separately from the split would help — but only by skipping a step
that is already nearly free to redo, while leaving the 51 minutes intact
whenever the spec IS edited, which is exactly when the cache misses.

* Why it is 51 minutes: the export is super-linear

Exporting prefixes of the same spec to texi, one Emacs run each:

| input            | lines  | sections | time |
|------------------+--------+----------+------|
| 1/40 of the spec |  2 378 |      536 |  1 s |
| 1/20             |  4 757 |    1 155 |  2 s |
| 1/10             |  9 514 |    2 212 |  6 s |
| 1/5              | 19 029 |    4 963 | 27 s |

Doubling the input roughly quadruples the time at the larger sizes. The
whole file is five times the last row and takes 51 minutes, so the growth
is at least quadratic over the range that matters.

That is the finding: the cost is not in *how many* pages there are, it is
in *how big one export is*.

* The reorganisation, tested

pjb's proposal was measured directly. The per-entry =.org= files already
exist — =build-paged-spec= emits them in 3.1 s — so the question is only
what it costs to export them individually instead of all at once:

: split:      3022 page .org files          (3.1 s, already a build step)
: individual: 100 pages exported in 2 s
: extrapolated to 3022 pages:  about 60 s

*51 minutes becomes about one minute* for a full rebuild, and for the
case pjb actually cares about — one page edited — it becomes one page's
export (~20 ms) plus a concatenation.

So the reorganisation is worth doing, for a better reason than the one it
was proposed for: not because the split is expensive (it is not), but
because N small exports of a quadratic algorithm beat one large one by
the square of the ratio.

* What still has to be verified before implementing

The measurement above exports 3022 INDEPENDENT texi files. A reader wants
ONE Info manual with working cross-references and menus, and that is the
part this issue has *not* proved:

1. each page exports as a standalone manual (=@setfilename=, its own node
   hierarchy). Assembling them means stripping those headers and
   splicing the bodies under a generated top-level node + =@menu=, or
   emitting =@include= files and letting =makeinfo= assemble;
2. cross-references between entries currently resolve because everything
   is in one export. Per-page exports must keep =@ref= targets valid
   across files -- texinfo supports it, org's exporter has to be made to
   emit it;
3. the same question applies to the HTML side, which already splits into
   3022 pages (2 min 38 s) and could be made incremental the same way,
   for a smaller but real gain.

Until 1 and 2 are settled, the 60 s figure is the cost of the exports
alone, not of a finished manual. It is a strong enough signal to justify
the work; it is not yet a promise of a 50x build.

* DONE so far — the cheap mitigation (2026-08-19, clautolisp 1.8.55)

pjb: « oui, fais le changement d'une ligne pour la lane de release ».

=SPEC_PAGED_TARGETS= (autolisp-spec/Makefile) names which paged
derivatives =paged= builds; it defaults to =pages html info=, and the
merge-request =documentation= job now passes =pages html=. The release
lane keeps the default. Nothing stops being built — it stops being
REBUILT on a path where nobody reads it: the Info manual is a release
product, and what a merge request needs checked is the HTML and the PDFs.

Effect: a merge request that edits the spec goes from about 55 minutes to
about 4. Verified with =make -n= from both the subproject and the repo
root, with and without the variable: the =build-paged-info= recipe
disappears with it and is present without it, and command-line variables
propagate through the sub-make, so the root invocation is enough.

*The skip is asserted, not assumed.* The install step is guarded by
=if [ -f … ]=, so a release whose Info build quietly failed would ship a
documentation artefact without the manual and say nothing — the exact
shape of failure this repository keeps meeting. release:documentation now
tests for =autolisp-spec/build/info/autolisp-spec.info= and fails if it
is absent. It is the one lane that publishes it, so it is the one lane
that must insist.

*This is scheduling, not a fix.* The export is still quadratic and still
takes 51 minutes when it runs; the release lane still pays it. The
reorganisation below is what would actually make it cheap, and this issue
stays open for it.

* A cheaper first step, if the reorganisation is not taken now

The =documentation= job's cache is keyed on the spec org file, so ANY
merge request that edits the spec pays the full 51 minutes -- and the two
that did (!104, !105) each did. Two mitigations, independent of the
redesign:

- give =build-paged-info= its own cache entry, keyed on the spec, so a
  branch that edits the spec pays once and re-runs of the same pipeline
  do not. It does not help the first run, which is the expensive one.
- or run the Info export only where the Info manual is actually
  published (the release lane), not on every MR. The HTML and the PDFs
  are what a merge request needs to have checked; the Info manual is a
  release product. This is the one-line change and takes the 51 minutes
  off the MR path entirely.

* Notes

Found 2026-08-19 while waiting for MR !104 / !105. The job LOOKS hung --
=org-texinfo-export-to-texinfo= writes nothing for the whole 51 minutes,
so the trace stops dead after "exporting … -> .texi" and the only way to
tell a slow export from a wedged one is to wait or to check a previous
job's duration. Whatever is done about the speed, that silence is worth
removing: a progress line per N sections would make the difference
visible from outside.
